const studentClass=`class Student
{
    public int StudentId { get; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Course { get; set; }
    public List<decimal> Grades { get; } = new();
    public decimal Average => Grades.Count == 0 ? 0m : Grades.Average();
    public Student(int studentId, string name, string course) { if (studentId <= 0 || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name)) throw new ArgumentException("Valid ID/name required"); StudentId = studentId; Name = name.Trim(); Course = course.Trim(); }
}`;
add('Classes, Objects, and Constructors','classes; objects; fields/properties/methods; constructors; this; instances; initialization; auto-properties',
 'A class describes state and behavior and an object is an instance. Prefer properties for a public contract; private fields store internal state. Constructors initialize valid instances and this refers to the current instance. An auto-property lets the compiler generate backing storage; validation may require a custom setter or method. The example constructor validates ID/name and initializes non-null strings. Its grade collection is pedagogical: the next lesson encapsulates mutation so invalid grades cannot be inserted through an exposed list.',
 program('var maria = new Student(1, "Maria", "IT");\nmaria.Grades.AddRange(new[] {95m, 89m, 91m});\nvar joel = new Student(2, "Joel", "CS");\nforeach (var student in new[] {maria, joel}) Console.WriteLine($"{student.StudentId}: {student.Name}; {student.Course}; average {student.Average:F2}");', '', 'using System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Linq;')+studentClass+'\n',
 'Create Student with ID, Name, Course, Grades and Average. Construct several objects and display their information. Demonstrate this, object initialization for suitable settable properties, and explain fields versus properties and no-grade handling.',
 [['What creates an instance?','new','using alone','return alone','public alone'],['What initializes valid object state?','A constructor','A namespace','A quiz','A CSV separator'],['What does this refer to?','The current instance','Every class at once','The production database','Only the parent class'],['What is an auto-property?','A property with compiler-generated backing storage','An automatically authorized user','A global variable','A trigger'],['Why encapsulate grade mutation?','Prevent invalid state','Make all values strings','Remove validation','Avoid constructors']]);
add('Encapsulation and Inheritance','public/private/protected/internal; fields; validated properties; getters/setters; base/derived classes; colon syntax; base; protected members; overriding',
 'Encapsulation protects invariants by controlling state changes. public exposes the contract, private limits a member to its declaring type, protected allows access from derived types, and internal limits access to the assembly. Inheritance models an is-a relationship and C# allows one direct base class. : names the base, base calls its constructor or accesses its member, and virtual/override permit runtime specialization. Prefer composition when reuse does not describe an is-a relationship. Expose read-only views and validated operations rather than mutable internals.',
 program('var student = new EnrolledStudent("Maria");\nstudent.AddGrade(91.5m);\nConsole.WriteLine($"{student.Name}: {student.Average:F2}");',`    class Person { public string Name { get; } public Person(string name) { if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name)) throw new ArgumentException("Name required"); Name = name.Trim(); } }
    class EnrolledStudent : Person { private readonly List<decimal> grades = new(); public EnrolledStudent(string name) : base(name) {} public decimal Average => grades.Count == 0 ? 0 : grades.Average(); public void AddGrade(decimal value) { if (value < 0 || value > 100) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(value)); grades.Add(value); } }
    class Teacher : Person { public Teacher(string name) : base(name) {} }`),
 'Create Person, Student and Teacher classes. Use validated constructors/properties, private grade storage and AddGrade; show base and a protected helper. Reject blank names and invalid grades and explain when inheritance versus composition is appropriate.',
 [['What protects a member within its type?','private','public','using','override'],['What can a derived type access?','Appropriate protected members','Every private field directly','Every production secret','Only static Main'],['How is a base class named?','After :','After SQL WHERE','With extends in C#','With a string literal'],['What initializes the parent?','base(...)','this always disables it','new on every property','foreach'],['What should a state-changing API enforce?','Its invariants','No validation','Public mutable fields only','Plaintext authentication']]);
add('Polymorphism, Abstract Classes, and Interfaces','virtual/override; runtime dispatch; abstract types/methods; interface contracts; multiple interfaces',
 'Polymorphism lets a base/interface reference invoke the concrete implementation. A virtual base member can be overridden; abstract members require implementation in non-abstract derived classes. An abstract class can share state and behavior but cannot be instantiated directly. Interfaces express contracts and a class can implement several interfaces. Prefer focused contracts such as IPrintable. Method hiding with new is different from override and is not used for runtime polymorphism here.',
 program('Person[] people = {new StudentPerson("Maria"), new TeacherPerson("Joel")};\nforeach (Person person in people) { Console.WriteLine(person.Describe()); ((IPrintable)person).Print(); }',`    interface IPrintable { void Print(); }
    abstract class Person { protected string Name { get; } protected Person(string name) { Name = name; } public abstract string Describe(); }
    class StudentPerson : Person, IPrintable { public StudentPerson(string name) : base(name) {} public override string Describe() => $"Student: {Name}"; public void Print() => Console.WriteLine("Student record"); }
    class TeacherPerson : Person, IPrintable { public TeacherPerson(string name) : base(name) {} public override string Describe() => $"Teacher: {Name}"; public void Print() => Console.WriteLine("Teacher record"); }`),
 'Create abstract Person, Student : Person and Teacher : Person plus IPrintable and a second focused interface. Process them through base/interface references; contrast virtual/override and abstract requirements.',
 [['What selects an override through a base reference?','Runtime polymorphism','Only variable spelling','SQL ordering','A password'],['Can an abstract class be instantiated directly?','No','Always','Only in a textarea','Only with a public field'],['What expresses a contract?','An interface','A raw secret','A CSV header','A loop counter'],['Can a class implement multiple interfaces?','Yes','No','Only two base classes instead','Only if all methods are static'],['What is needed for an overridden base method?','virtual or abstract (or an inherited override)','A matching comment','A public field only','A SELECT query']]);
const fileNotes='Use a disposable working directory. Fixed relative file names are application choices, not an OS security boundary. A verified online runner must restrict filesystem access at OS/container level, cap file size, and destroy the entire workspace. Do not upload production files or use user-supplied paths. Real CSV supports quoting and delimiters; the simple line/JSON formats here are not a general CSV parser.';
